;;; A kernel dunder among the blind enumerator's objects, on the shipped
;;; stdlib — regression `arith-closing-rule`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `std.math.arith` imported whole, beside a relation the program declares
;;; and does not close, `total`. The module closes its own relations by its
;;; closing rule and writes no `(__closed__ R)` fact; the program writes no
;;; name at all.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program states no `(solve …)`, so its hypotheses are the facts of its
;;; open declared relation over its names, and it has none: `__closed__` is
;;; the kernel's word and no object. No candidate, and R is {∅}, one state.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One candidate, `(total __closed__)`, and R two states: the head of the
;;; facts the module's closing rule derives is in the KB's names index and in
;;; no registry, so the blind enumerator took it for an object. It leaves the
;;; kernel's dunder out by name now, and the answer is the one above.

(import std.math.arith)
(relation total Number :why "the total is {?1}")
(query :goal (total ?x))
